※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=30 diag=none

※ An array whose type does not say how long it is, `u6⟦⟧`, is where its elements
※ are and how many there are.  It owns nothing: the elements are an array's, or
※ part of one.
※
※ `a⟦i…j⟧` takes a run of them, from one place up to another -- the same
※ half-open convention a range has everywhere else, so that the count is the
※ difference between the ends.
※
※ An array whose type says how long it is stands where one that does not is
※ wanted: the elements are where they were and how many there are is what its
※ type said.  That goes one way only, since nothing that has lost its count can
※ promise to have four.

fn total(xs: u6⟦⟧, n: u6) → u6:
    let sum: mut u6 = 0u6
    foreach i := 0u6…n:
        sum ← sum + xs⟦i⟧
    sum

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let a: u6⟦6⟧ = ⟦1u6, 2u6, 4u6, 8u6, 16u6, 32u6⟧
    ※ four of them, starting at one
    total(a⟦1…5⟧, 4u6)
